---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/okad-house.css';
---

<Base title="Video Ads for E-commerce — Creative Testing at Scale | OKAD" description="Performance video ads for DTC and e-commerce brands. UGC, product demos, testimonials. Testing frameworks that find winners fast.">
  <div class="okh">

    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="khero" style="padding:80px 0 48px">
      <div class="w" style="max-width:900px">
        <span class="kick">Video Ads for E-commerce & DTC Brands</span>
        <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.2rem);margin-bottom:16px">E-commerce Video Ads That <span class="cap">Find Winners Fast</span></h1>
        <p class="lead">Most e-commerce brands waste budget running the same three ads until they burn out. We take a different approach: produce high volumes of performance creative, test systematically across hooks and formats, and scale what actually converts. You get a pipeline of fresh ads engineered for Meta, TikTok, and YouTube — not a single hero video you pray works.</p>
        <div class="row" style="margin-top:24px">
          <a class="btn" href="https://calendly.com/okad_calls/first" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book a call</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="mailto:olga@okad.agency">Email us</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHY VIDEO ADS FOR E-COMMERCE -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:16px">Why Video Ads Are the <span class="cap g">Growth Engine</span> for E-commerce</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin-bottom:24px;max-width:680px">Static images built the first wave of DTC brands. Video is building the next one. Platforms are prioritizing video in their algorithms, consumers expect to see a product in action before they buy, and the brands winning on paid social are the ones producing 20-50 new video concepts every month. If your creative strategy is still built around carousel ads and lifestyle photography, you are leaving money on the table.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:16px">
        <div style="padding:28px;background:#f4f9e8;border-radius:14px">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px;color:#111">Higher Conversion Rates</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0">Video ads consistently outperform static ads on conversion rate because they compress the entire sales pitch into 15-30 seconds. A customer sees the product, understands how it works, hears social proof, and gets a reason to buy — all before they even click. For physical products especially, video bridges the gap between browsing online and holding something in your hands. Brands switching from static-only to a video-first creative strategy typically see a 25-40% lift in conversion rate within the first 60 days.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:28px;background:#f3e5f5;border-radius:14px">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px;color:#111">Lower CPAs Through Testing</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0">The real advantage of video is not that any single ad performs better — it is that video gives you more levers to test. You can test different hooks, different creators, different product angles, different CTAs, all within the same concept. When you are running 15-20 ads per sprint instead of 3, you find winners faster and kill losers before they drain your budget. Volume testing compounds over time: each round teaches you what your audience responds to, making every subsequent batch more efficient.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:28px;background:#e1f5fe;border-radius:14px">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px;color:#111">Social Proof at Scale</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0">Reviews and star ratings are powerful, but a real person on camera saying "I was skeptical but this actually changed my routine" is on another level. UGC testimonial ads let you deploy social proof as a paid acquisition tool, not just a landing page element. The key is volume: one testimonial ad will fatigue. Ten different creators telling ten different stories about ten different use cases gives you coverage across audience segments and keeps your campaigns fresh for months.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:28px;background:#f6f5f4;border-radius:14px">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px;color:#111">Platform Algorithm Advantage</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0">Meta, TikTok, and YouTube all give preferential treatment to video content in their ad delivery systems. On Meta, Reels placements consistently deliver 20-35% lower CPMs than feed placements. TikTok is video-native by design, so static ads look out of place and underperform. YouTube Shorts is the fastest-growing ad placement on the platform. Brands that produce platform-native video — vertical, captioned, fast-paced — access cheaper inventory and better delivery optimization than brands stuck on static.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- AD TYPES -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:8px">Ad Formats That <span class="cap y">Convert</span> for E-commerce</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin-bottom:24px;max-width:680px">Different stages of the funnel need different types of creative. We produce all of these formats and test them against each other to find what works for your specific product and audience.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:16px">
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">UGC Testimonial</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">Real customers talking about your product with the product in hand. This is the workhorse format for cold traffic acquisition because it combines social proof with product demonstration in a single ad. The best testimonial ads do not feel scripted — the creator has actually used the product and shares a specific moment or result. We typically produce 5-8 testimonial variations per product to test different creator demographics, use cases, and hook styles.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Product Demo / How-To</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">Showing your product in use, step by step. Demos work best for products with a visual transformation or a usage process that is not immediately obvious — skincare routines, kitchen gadgets, fitness equipment, tech accessories. The key is to show the product solving a real problem, not just existing. These ads tend to have the highest watch-through rates because viewers stay to see the result, and they work especially well in retargeting where the viewer already knows the brand.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Comparison / VS</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">Your product versus a competitor, the old way versus the new way, or the cheap option versus yours. Comparison ads are one of the most effective formats for mid-funnel audiences who are already considering a purchase but have not decided which brand to buy from. The structure creates an inherent narrative tension — the viewer wants to see which one wins. These work particularly well for products in crowded categories where differentiation matters: supplements, skincare, home goods, and consumer electronics.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Unboxing & First Impression</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">The reveal moment when a package arrives and someone opens it for the first time. Unboxing ads tap into the universal anticipation of getting something new — viewers live vicariously through the experience. This format is ideal for subscription boxes, premium packaging, gift-worthy products, and anything with a strong visual first impression. The most effective unboxing ads include a genuine reaction shot and end with the creator using the product, not just admiring the box.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Story-Driven</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">Problem-agitation-solution narrative arcs that take the viewer on a journey. The creator describes a problem they had, shows how frustrating it was, then reveals how your product solved it. This format works because it mirrors the buyer's own internal dialogue — they recognize the problem, feel the pain, and then see the solution. Story-driven ads tend to perform best with higher-priced products where the purchase decision involves more consideration: mattresses, furniture, premium supplements, high-end beauty.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Founder Story</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">The brand origin story told by the founder: why they built the product, what problem they were solving, what makes their approach different. Founder-led ads humanize the brand and build trust in ways that polished brand content cannot. They work best for brands with a genuine mission or a founder with a compelling personal connection to the product. We see founder story ads consistently outperform other formats for brands in the $50-200 AOV range where trust and brand narrative influence purchase decisions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- HOW WE TEST -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:16px">How We <span class="cap v">Test</span> Creative at Scale</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin-bottom:28px;max-width:680px">Most agencies produce a batch of ads and hope for the best. We run a structured testing framework designed to find winners quickly, learn from losers, and compound performance over time.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;gap:24px">
        <div style="padding:28px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:14px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px;color:#111">Test More Concepts, Not Just More Variations</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">The biggest mistake in creative testing is spending all your budget testing variations of one idea — different thumbnails, different CTAs, different colors on the same concept. That is not testing. Real creative testing means testing fundamentally different concepts: different angles, different creator types, different formats, different hooks. We typically test 15-20 distinct ads per sprint across 4-5 different concepts. This gives you statistically meaningful data on what resonates with your audience, not just what shade of blue gets more clicks. Each concept addresses a different purchase motivation — price, quality, convenience, social proof, aspirational identity — so you learn which levers move your specific customer.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:28px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:14px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px;color:#111">Hook Testing: 3-5 Different Openings Per Concept</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">The first three seconds determine whether your ad gets watched or skipped. For every winning concept, we produce 3-5 different hooks to test which opening captures attention best. Our hook categories include: pattern interrupt (something visually unexpected that stops the scroll), direct question ("Are you still using X to do Y?"), statistic lead ("87% of people do this wrong"), bold claim ("This replaced four products in my routine"), and visual hook (an eye-catching product shot or transformation before any words). Hook testing alone can improve a winning ad's performance by 30-50% because you are optimizing the highest-leverage moment in the entire ad.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:28px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:14px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px;color:#111">The Iteration Framework: Learning from Losers</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">A losing ad is not wasted spend — it is data. Every ad that does not convert teaches you something about what your audience does not respond to. We categorize every tested ad into a performance database and tag it by concept angle, hook type, creator demographic, format, and CTA. After each sprint, we run a structured analysis: which hooks got the highest hold rates, which concepts drove the most clicks, which creator profiles generated the lowest CPA. This analysis directly informs the brief for the next sprint. Over 3-4 months, this compounding learning effect typically reduces average CPA by 30-40% compared to the first sprint because each round is built on real data, not guesswork.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:28px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:14px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px;color:#111">Scaling Winners: Extending the Life of What Works</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">When we find a winning ad — one that hits your CPA target with consistent performance — we do not just increase the budget and hope it holds. We create 4-6 variations designed to extend its lifespan: same concept with a different creator, same script with a different hook, same hook with a different visual style, same story reshot in a different setting. This approach lets you scale spend on a proven concept while fighting creative fatigue. A single winning concept can generate 8-12 weeks of consistent performance when you rotate variations strategically, compared to the typical 2-3 week lifespan of a single ad at scale.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- RESULTS & CASE STUDIES -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:8px">Results That <span class="cap p">Speak</span> for Themselves</h2>
      <p style="color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin-bottom:24px;max-width:680px">Here is what happens when you pair high-volume creative production with structured testing and rapid iteration.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:16px">
        <div style="padding:28px;background:#f4f9e8;border-radius:14px">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.05em">DTC Skincare Brand</span>
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin:10px 0 12px;color:#111">40% CPA Reduction in 90 Days</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0 0 12px"><strong style="color:#111">Challenge:</strong> This skincare brand was spending $80K/month on Meta ads but relied on three hero videos that had been running for six months. Creative fatigue was pushing CPAs up 15-20% month over month, and the team had no system for producing and testing new creative.</p>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0 0 12px"><strong style="color:#111">Approach:</strong> We produced 45 ads in the first sprint across five concept angles: before/after transformations, dermatologist-style explainers, morning routine integrations, ingredient deep-dives, and real customer testimonials. Each concept had 3 hook variations. We tested on $200/day per ad for 5 days before making cut decisions.</p>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0"><strong style="color:#111">Result:</strong> Found 6 winning ads in the first month, scaled to 160+ total ads produced over the quarter. CPA dropped from $38 to $23 — a 40% reduction. The morning routine format with a question hook became their top performer for 8 consecutive weeks.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:28px;background:#e1f5fe;border-radius:14px">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.05em">Subscription Box Company</span>
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin:10px 0 12px;color:#111">2.1x ROAS Improvement</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0 0 12px"><strong style="color:#111">Challenge:</strong> A monthly subscription box for pet owners was stuck at 1.4x ROAS on Meta and could not profitably scale past $30K/month in ad spend. Their creative was all polished brand content — product flat lays and studio shoots — that looked like ads and got scrolled past.</p>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0 0 12px"><strong style="color:#111">Approach:</strong> We shifted to a UGC-first strategy with real pet owners filming unboxing reactions — both the human reaction and the pet's reaction to new toys and treats. We produced 30 ads per month with a mix of unboxing reveals, pet reaction compilations, and "what's in the box" explainer formats. Each month we tested new hooks based on previous learnings.</p>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0"><strong style="color:#111">Result:</strong> ROAS climbed from 1.4x to 2.9x over four months. The pet reaction format outperformed all other concepts by 3x. Monthly ad spend scaled from $30K to $85K while maintaining profitability. The brand now produces 25-35 new UGC ads every month through our pipeline.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:28px;background:#f3e5f5;border-radius:14px">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.05em">DTC Home Goods Brand</span>
          <h3 style="font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin:10px 0 12px;color:#111">$12 CPA on a $95 AOV Product</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0 0 12px"><strong style="color:#111">Challenge:</strong> A home goods brand selling a premium kitchen product at $95 needed to lower acquisition costs to fund expansion into new markets. Previous agencies had produced beautiful brand content that generated engagement but not purchases. CPA was hovering around $32.</p>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0 0 12px"><strong style="color:#111">Approach:</strong> We identified three angles through competitor research and review mining: the time-saving angle, the comparison angle (old way vs. this product), and the "I didn't know I needed this" discovery angle. We produced 20 ads in the first sprint with 5 creators across different demographics. We tested aggressively on both Meta and TikTok simultaneously.</p>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:#4d4d49;margin:0"><strong style="color:#111">Result:</strong> The comparison format crushed on TikTok — one ad drove a $9 CPA for three consecutive weeks. Average CPA across all winning ads settled at $12, down from $32. The brand scaled TikTok spend from $0 to $45K/month profitably, and the winning concepts were adapted for Meta where they maintained a $15 CPA.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- OUR PROCESS -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:24px">Our <span class="cap d">Process</span></h2>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(220px,1fr));gap:20px">
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);letter-spacing:0.05em">01</span>
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:17px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin:8px 0 6px">Creative Strategy & Audit</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">We start by auditing your current ad creative, analyzing what has worked and what has not. We pull apart your competitors' top-performing ads using ad libraries and spy tools. We review your product pages, customer reviews, and brand positioning to identify untapped messaging angles. The output is a creative strategy document mapping specific concepts to funnel stages.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);letter-spacing:0.05em">02</span>
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:17px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin:8px 0 6px">Concept Development</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">We write full scripts for each concept, including multiple hook options, body structure, and CTA variations. Each script is mapped to a specific funnel stage: top-of-funnel awareness, mid-funnel consideration, or bottom-funnel conversion. We select visual approaches — UGC talking head, product demo, lifestyle vignette, or hybrid — and match each concept with the right creator profile from our roster of 600+ vetted creators.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);letter-spacing:0.05em">03</span>
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:17px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin:8px 0 6px">Production Sprint</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">Creators film against our briefs while our editing team prepares templates and motion graphics. All footage is edited into platform-native formats — 9:16 for Reels, Stories, and TikTok, 4:5 for feed, 16:9 for YouTube. Every ad gets captions, brand-consistent lower thirds, and optimized pacing. We deliver first drafts within 48 hours of receiving raw footage, with one round of revisions included. A typical sprint produces 15-20 finished ads.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted,#8e8e8e);letter-spacing:0.05em">04</span>
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:17px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin:8px 0 6px">Analysis & Iteration</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">After each testing period, we review performance data across every ad: CTR, hold rate, CPA, ROAS, and thumb-stop ratio. We categorize results to identify winning concepts, winning hooks, and winning creator profiles. Losers get analyzed for why they failed — was it the hook, the concept, the creator, or the CTA? These learnings feed directly into the brief for the next sprint, creating a compounding improvement cycle that gets smarter with every round.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- STATS ROW -->
    <section style="padding:64px 24px;background:var(--stage,#111)">
      <div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:24px;text-align:center">
        <div>
          <b style="display:block;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.2rem);font-weight:700;color:#b6f36b;margin-bottom:6px">200+</b>
          <span style="font-size:14px;color:#8e8e8e">DTC brands served</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <b style="display:block;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.2rem);font-weight:700;color:#b6f36b;margin-bottom:6px">160+</b>
          <span style="font-size:14px;color:#8e8e8e">Ads per brand avg</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <b style="display:block;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.2rem);font-weight:700;color:#b6f36b;margin-bottom:6px">40%</b>
          <span style="font-size:14px;color:#8e8e8e">Avg CPA reduction</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <b style="display:block;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.2rem);font-weight:700;color:#b6f36b;margin-bottom:6px">48hr</b>
          <span style="font-size:14px;color:#8e8e8e">First drafts</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <style>
        @media(max-width:600px){
          .okh section [style*="grid-template-columns:repeat(4"] {
            grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr !important;
            gap: 32px 16px !important;
          }
        }
      </style>
    </section>

    <!-- BEST PRACTICES -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:8px">E-commerce Video Ad <span class="cap g">Best Practices</span></h2>
      <p style="color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin-bottom:24px;max-width:680px">Actionable advice from producing thousands of e-commerce video ads. These are the principles that separate ads that scale from ads that waste budget.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;gap:20px">
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Win the First 3 Seconds or Lose Everything</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">On average, 65% of viewers drop off within the first three seconds of a video ad. This means your hook is not "part of the ad" — it is the ad. Start with movement, a provocative statement, a relatable question, or a visual surprise. Never open with your logo, a slow pan, or "Hey guys." Test at least 3 different hooks for every concept. The difference between a good hook and a great hook can be the difference between a $30 CPA and a $15 CPA. Analyze your thumb-stop rate (3-second video plays divided by impressions) — anything below 25% means your hook needs work.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Aspect Ratios Are Not Optional</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">Each platform and placement has an optimal aspect ratio, and using the wrong one means your ad looks like it does not belong. For TikTok and Instagram Reels, 9:16 vertical is mandatory — a letterboxed 16:9 video will never compete. For Meta feed placements, 4:5 is the sweet spot because it takes up the most screen real estate in the feed. For YouTube in-stream, 16:9 remains standard. Always produce your hero creative in the primary ratio for your target platform, then adapt for secondary placements. Do not crop — re-edit to ensure the framing works natively in each ratio.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">How to Brief UGC Creators</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">The brief is the most underrated part of the creative process. A vague brief ("talk about why you love the product") produces generic content. A good brief gives the creator a specific hook, a clear structure (hook, problem, solution, CTA), 2-3 talking points to hit, and freedom to deliver it in their own voice. Include reference videos showing the energy and style you want. Specify the setting — natural light in a kitchen, gym, bathroom. Tell them what NOT to say. And always send the product early enough that they have actually used it before filming. The difference between a briefed and an unbriefed creator is the difference between an ad and a random video.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Testing Budgets: How Much You Actually Need</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">You need enough spend per ad to reach statistical significance, but not so much that you waste budget on obvious losers. Our rule of thumb: allocate $150-200 per ad for a 3-5 day test on Meta (enough for roughly 10,000-15,000 impressions depending on your CPM). On TikTok, $100-150 per ad is usually sufficient due to lower CPMs. If an ad has not shown signal within 2x your target CPA in spend, kill it. If it is within striking distance, give it another day. For a sprint of 15 ads, budget $2,000-3,000 for testing alone — this is separate from your scaling budget. Brands that under-invest in testing end up scaling mediocre creative and wonder why performance degrades.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">When to Kill Underperformers</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">Kill decisions should be data-driven, not emotional. After spending 1.5-2x your target CPA on an ad with zero conversions, turn it off — it is not going to magically start working. If an ad has a high CTR but no conversions, the problem is likely your landing page or offer, not the ad. If an ad has low CTR but the few people who click convert well, the hook needs work but the concept has potential — test new hooks. Do not kill ads during the learning phase (first 24-48 hours on Meta). And do not kill ads based on one bad day — look at 3-5 day trends before making final decisions. The goal is not to find perfect ads; it is to find ads that are good enough to scale.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <h3 style="font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;color:#111">Structuring Creative Sprints</h3>
          <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin:0">A creative sprint should run on a 2-week cycle: week one for strategy, scripting, filming, and editing; week two for testing, analysis, and briefing the next sprint. This cadence keeps your ad account consistently fed with fresh creative and prevents the feast-or-famine cycle where you produce a big batch, run it until it dies, then scramble to make more. Each sprint should include a mix of net-new concepts (60%) and iterations on previous winners (40%). Track everything in a shared spreadsheet: concept angle, hook type, creator, format, CPA, ROAS, and notes on why it worked or failed. After 3-4 sprints, you will have a creative playbook specific to your brand that makes every subsequent batch more effective.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section class="faq" style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:24px">Frequently Asked <span class="cap y">Questions</span></h2>

      <details>
        <summary>How much do e-commerce video ads cost?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>Our pricing depends on the volume and complexity of what you need. A starter package of 10 video ads starts at $500 and includes strategy, scripting, creator matching, filming, and editing. For brands running ongoing creative testing programs, we offer monthly retainers starting at $2,000/month for 20-30 ads. Enterprise packages for brands spending $100K+ on paid social include dedicated creative strategists, priority turnaround, and custom reporting. Every package includes platform-native formatting (9:16, 4:5, 16:9), captions, and one round of revisions. We price per deliverable, not per hour, so you always know what you are paying upfront.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>How many ads do I need to test to find a winner?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>Based on our data across hundreds of brands, you typically need to test 10-15 ads to find 2-3 that hit your performance targets. That is roughly a 15-20% win rate, which is strong by industry standards — most brands testing on their own see 5-10% win rates because they do not have a systematic approach to concept development. We recommend starting with a sprint of 15-20 ads across 4-5 different concepts. This gives you enough data to identify which concept angles and hook types resonate with your audience. From there, each subsequent sprint gets more efficient because you are building on real performance data instead of guessing.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What platforms do you produce video ads for?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>We produce ads optimized for Meta (Facebook and Instagram, including Reels, Stories, and Feed placements), TikTok (In-Feed, Spark Ads, and TopView), YouTube (Shorts, In-Stream, and Discovery), Pinterest (Video Pins and Idea Pins), and Snapchat (Story Ads and Commercials). Each platform has different creative best practices — what works on TikTok often falls flat on Meta, and vice versa. We produce platform-native content for each, which means we do not just resize the same video. We adjust pacing, hook style, caption formatting, and CTA placement to match what performs on each platform. Most of our clients start with Meta and TikTok, then expand to YouTube Shorts once they have winning concepts.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What is the turnaround time for a batch of ads?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>From kickoff to delivery, a standard sprint takes 7-10 business days. That breaks down to 2 days for strategy and scripting, 3-4 days for creator filming, and 2-3 days for editing and revisions. First drafts are delivered within 48 hours of receiving raw footage from creators. For rush orders, we can deliver a batch of 10 ads in 5 business days with a 20% rush fee. Ongoing retainer clients get priority scheduling, which means their sprints typically run 1-2 days faster than project-based clients. We also offer an "always-on" model for high-volume clients where production is continuous — as one sprint delivers, the next is already in filming.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Do you handle media buying and ad management too?</summary>
        <div class="ans">
          <p>We focus on creative production and creative strategy, not media buying. We have found that specialization produces better results — our team is obsessed with making great ads, and we partner with media buyers and agencies who are obsessed with distributing them efficiently. That said, we work closely with your media buying team or agency to ensure our creative is structured for their testing framework. We provide naming conventions, audience recommendations, and suggested testing budgets for every batch. If you do not have a media buyer, we can recommend trusted partners from our network who specialize in e-commerce paid social. We also provide creative performance analysis after each testing period so you get strategic input even though we are not managing the ad spend directly.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

